  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Find the coefficient of $(x+1)^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{-x}$ about $x=-1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe coefficient of $(x+1)^{3}$ is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The coefficient of $(x+1)^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{\u2034}(-1).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =-{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{\u2034}(-1),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2034}(-1)=-{e}^{-(-1)}=-e.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{\u2034}(-1)=\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-e)=-\\frac{e}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of $(x+1)^{3}$ is $-\\frac{e}{6}.$",
